# Quality

This document is the project-owned quality authority. It names the checks that establish acceptance, the evidence those checks must produce, and the gaps that remain. It does not install or operate a separate verification service.

## Required checks and commands

{{QUALITY_EVIDENCE}}

Project maintainers own the exact test, lint, type, build, distribution, smoke, security, and documentation commands for this repository. Record commands and their results here or in the applicable Plan; do not replace a named project check with an inferred command or an Agent summary. Setup handoff does not execute project commands.

## Evidence contract

For each acceptance criterion, record:

- the exact project-owned check or observation;
- the candidate identity and relevant baseline;
- the result, including failures and not-run checks;
- the artifact, log, path, or reviewer observation that supports the result; and
- the remaining risk, follow-up, or reason a check could not run.

A Verification Sub starts from a fresh context, identifies the integrated candidate, treats implementation narration as unverified, runs applicable project-owned checks, and returns criterion-level results. Verification is read-only, nonrepairing, and nondelegating. Candidate changes invalidate earlier verification evidence.

## Quality ownership and handoff

Main owns acceptance criteria, evidence judgment, integration, and terminal Plan movement. An Implementation Sub receives one bounded Task Packet and returns changes plus evidence; it does not approve its own work. A broad or multi-part root defaults to `Role: Task Owner` with `May delegate: yes`, while a narrow or inherently serial root remains a direct nondelegating leaf.

For a broad root, the shared host/project topology is:

`T<n> (broad root Owner) -> T<n>-A/B/C/... (declared child packets, all ready leaves dispatched concurrently) -> T<n>-I (Owner-local aggregation) -> T<n>-V1/V2/... (parallel fresh verification)`

Each Owner first returns a finite child manifest. Main alone serializes accepted child rows and complete matching packets into the Plan and resumes that Owner. Only the resumed Owner dispatches dependency-ready descendants. Descendants inherit scope, acceptance, non-goals, protected paths, child budget, and frozen interfaces. Mutable siblings require disjoint allowed-write sets and separate exact-baseline worktrees. Owner-local aggregation is distinct from Main's final repository integration.

## Generated-target quality boundary

After explicit setup handoff, the target remains useful without a package. Its quality boundary is ordinary Markdown, project source and tests, project-owned commands, CI, Git, and visible evidence. No target runtime, scheduler, dispatcher, task database, command runner, Gate, evidence archive, automatic closure, generated settings, marker, role or procedure Skill, command wrapper, or hidden state is required. No generated authority directs an Agent to locate, install, resolve, import, invoke, or verify Reporivet or a package diagnosis command.

## Capability artifact verification

`docs/DESIGN.md` remains the universal visual, interaction, and accessibility authority. If `web_ui=yes` is explicitly Confirmed, `docs/FRONTEND.md` is a project-owned implementation and client-side loading/error/retry artifact; if `deployed_runtime=yes` is explicitly Confirmed, `docs/RELIABILITY.md` is a project-owned service/runtime reliability supplement subordinate to `docs/OPERATIONS.md`. Neither optional document changes this quality authority or creates a verification service.

A capability file is not evidence merely because it exists. Keep missing, Proposed, Open, Sources-only, inferred, and unverified values visibly distinct, and keep audit observations separate from setup selection and Plan state. For frontend work, name exact project-owned UI checks; for runtime reliability work, name exact project-owned operational observations and checks. Record candidate identity, command or observation, result, supporting artifact, provenance, and residual risk in the active Plan or applicable runbook. A fresh Verification Sub checks the integrated candidate read-only; this document remains the route for deciding what project-owned verification establishes acceptance.

## Static runbook quality

A static runbook is produced only from one complete, unique, user-confirmed strict structured procedure record in `Confirmed`. The exact nine fields are `slug`, `title`, `trigger`, `reads`, `actions`, `stop_conditions`, `evidence`, `permissions`, and `rollback`; no field, alias, value, or default may be inferred. The result is ordinary Markdown at `docs/runbooks/<slug>.md` and carries no frontmatter, executor metadata, hooks, command registration, or privilege-bearing configuration. Incomplete, malformed, generic, inferred, Proposed, Open, Sources-only, or duplicate records produce no runbook.

## Quality gaps

Keep unavailable or uncertain checks in `Open`. Record a bounded follow-up in the active Plan or `docs/exec-plans/tech-debt-tracker.md` when no approved current change can resolve it. Do not turn scanner inference, historical output, or a passing unrelated command into current acceptance evidence.

## Change triggers

Update this document when project commands, supported environments, acceptance criteria, evidence retention, CI, release checks, or verification boundaries change. Link the governing product spec, design, decision, or completed Plan rather than copying historical rationale into this current summary.
